meta {
  name: Change User Roles
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users/{{invitedUserId}}/roles
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "roleIds": ["{{inviteRoleId}}"]
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns the populated roles", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.roleIds).to.be.an("array").with.lengthOf(1);
    expect(body.roleIds[0]).to.have.property("slug");
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /users/:userId/roles — replace a user's roles

  **Module:** none (`@SkipModule()`).
  **Permission:** `agency:users:write`

  Runs against the **invited** row from *Invite User*, before *Revoke Invite*
  deletes it — deliberately not against the producer, whose `own` data scope the
  Leads / Performance / Leaderboard folders depend on.

  A full replacement, not a merge. A user may hold several roles: their
  permissions union, and their data scope collapses to the **widest** of the
  roles held — so pairing Producer (`own`) with Data Team (`agency`) gives agency
  scope on everything.

  The assignment lives in the `userRoles` collection. The response still returns
  `roleIds` as populated `{ _id, name, slug }` objects, unchanged from when it
  was an array on the user document.

  ## Owner protection

  | Situation | Result |
  |---|---|
  | Adding the owner role to anyone | Allowed — this is how a second owner comes to exist. |
  | An owner giving up **their own** owner role, with another owner present | Allowed. |
  | An owner giving up their own owner role as the **last** owner | `409` — the agency would have nobody able to administer it. |
  | Taking the owner role off **someone else** | `403` — platform administrators only. |

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — a role id that is not a valid ObjectId, or belongs to
    another agency.
  - `403 Forbidden` — missing `agency:users:write`, or the owner rule above.
  - `404 Not Found` — no such user in this agency.
  - `409 Conflict` — the last-owner rule.
}
